    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d9">

                <pb facs="0021" n="11" sameAs="frus:frus1949v08/pg11"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/1–549: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Cabot</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-01-05T15:00:00+08:00">January 5, 1949—3
                            p. m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 5—12:07 p. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>27. Local press reports Shanghai Self-Help and National Salvation Association
                    sent identical telegrams January 4 to Central Govt and CCP headquarters Yenan,
                    appealing for termination hostilities and calling on both sides to send
                    delegates to Shanghai to “begin sincere consultations under the unbiased
                    observation of the Chinese people and of representatives of friendly nations, so
                    that all outstanding issues may be settled by political means”. General purport
                    this same as that of telegrams sent by Shanghai City Council preceding day
                    (remytel to Dept 22, 5th, repeated to Nanking 18). Telegrams appear to be part
                    of general peace offensive on part of C–C clique controlled <pb facs="0022"
                        n="12" xml:id="pg12"/> forces Shanghai for purpose throwing onus on
                    Communists if latter refuse comply with what is meant to appear to be
                    overwhelming popular demand for peace based on terms in Gimo’s<anchor n="30"
                        xml:id="d9-f1"/> New Year declaration.<anchor n="31" xml:id="d9-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>Sent Dept 27, pouched Nanking.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cabot</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d9-f1" n="30" xml:id="d9-f1-content">
                <p>Generalissimo Chiang Kai-shek, President of the Republic of China.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d9-f2" n="31" xml:id="d9-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram No. 34, January 6, 8 a. m., the Consul General at Shanghai reported
                    similar telegrams sent by business, labor, and educational organizations
                    (893.00/1–649).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
